---
// Reference / Compatibility and versioning.
//
// Four version questions that are genuinely independent of each other, which is
// why "what version is Strata" is not a useful question on its own. A reader
// wants to know whether their binary opens their database, whether their SDK
// matches their engine, and which interfaces are real.
//
// The matrix renders from the data the build already fetches: the engine
// version and platform targets from release.json, the Python SDK version from
// python-sdk.json, which fetch-python-bindings.mjs writes from the installed
// wheel. Hand-typing either is how the homepage came to advertise 1.1.1 while
// the docs said 1.2.2.
//
// The on-disk format facts are from the source rather than observation:
// DATABASE_FORMAT_VERSION in crates/storage/src/format/mod.rs, and the refusal
// arms in format/manifest.rs.
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import release from '../../../data/release.json';
import pythonSdk from '../../../data/python-sdk.json';
import index from '../../../data/command-index.json';

const engine = release.version;
const sdk = pythonSdk.version;
const sdkMatches = sdk === engine;
const commandCount = index.commands.length;
const platforms = [...new Set(release.targets.map((t) => t.target))].sort();

const clients = [
  {
    name: 'CLI',
    status: 'Supported',
    version: engine,
    note: 'Ships with the engine, so it is never a separate version.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Python',
    status: 'Supported',
    version: sdk ?? 'unknown',
    note: sdkMatches
      ? 'Matches the engine today.'
      : `Trails the engine (${engine}). Published separately, so the two can diverge for a few days after a release.`,
  },
  {
    name: 'MCP',
    status: 'Supported',
    version: engine,
    note: 'Built into the binary; no separate install.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Node.js',
    status: 'Not shipping',
    version: '0.15.0 on npm',
    note: 'That package predates 1.0 and is not maintained against the current engine. Do not install it.',
  },
  { name: 'Rust', status: 'Planned', version: '-', note: 'No published crate.' },
];
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Compatibility and versioning"
  description="Which binary opens which database, which SDK matches which engine, which clients are real, and what a research preview does and does not promise."
  section="Reference"
>
  <h1>Compatibility and versioning</h1>

  <p>
    There are four version questions here and they are independent of one another, which is why
    "what version of Strata are you on" is rarely enough to answer a compatibility problem. The
    binary, the on-disk format, the SDK and the interface each move on their own schedule.
  </p>

  <p>
    Strata is a research preview. That makes this page more important rather than less: instability
    is tolerable when you are told where it lives.
  </p>

  <h2>The current set</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Interface</th><th>Status</th><th>Version</th><th></th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {
        clients.map((c) => (
          <tr>
            <td>{c.name}</td>
            <td>{c.status}</td>
            <td>
              <code>{c.version}</code>
            </td>
            <td>{c.note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))
      }
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>
    Engine and CLI are at <code>{engine}</code>, carrying {commandCount} commands, built for {
      platforms.length
    } platform targets. Those numbers come from the release this site was built against, not from a list
    kept by hand.
  </p>

  <h2>Can this binary open this database?</h2>

  <p>
    The on-disk format has its own version, currently <strong>1</strong>, and it is not the same
    number as the binary. Every 1.x release so far reads and writes format 1, so upgrading the
    binary does not migrate anything.
  </p>

  <p>
    A binary that meets a format it does not understand refuses it rather than guessing. A database
    written by some future format is rejected as a future format, naming the highest version this
    binary supports. A pre-1.0 development database is rejected as well, deliberately and by a
    distinct path, so an old experiment cannot be silently half-read. The manifest carries a magic
    marker and a CRC, so a truncated or corrupted one is caught at open rather than surfacing later
    as wrong data.
  </p>

  <p>
    That is the forward-compatibility answer, and it is the conservative one: <strong>
      an older binary will not open a newer database, and will say so.
    </strong> There is no partial read.
  </p>

  <h2>Does this SDK match this engine?</h2>

  {
    sdkMatches ? (
      <p>
        The Python SDK is at <code>{sdk}</code>, the same as the engine. Keep them together: the
        wheel and the binary are published separately, and they can drift for a few days after a
        release while one waits for the other.
      </p>
    ) : (
      <p>
        The Python SDK is at <code>{sdk}</code> and the engine is at <code>{engine}</code>. They are
        published separately and this window happens after most releases. The SDK talks to the
        engine it is loaded against, so the practical effect is that commands added in{' '}
        <code>{engine}</code> may have no Python method yet.
      </p>
    )
  }

  <p>
    {pythonSdk.commands_without_binding} of the {commandCount} commands have no Python method today. Every
    command page in the <a href="/docs/reference">reference</a> shows the Python spelling when one exists
    and says so when it does not, generated from the installed wheel rather than written down.
  </p>

  <h2>Two processes, two versions</h2>

  <p>
    One case is worth knowing before you hit it. When a process brokers to a host, the two negotiate
    on a protocol revision, and <strong
      >the pairing is not refused when their releases differ</strong
    >
    . Between 1.2.1 and 1.2.2 the revision is the same number on both sides, so the connection is accepted,
    and one specific read fails at the point of use rather than at connect: a 1.2.2 client reading a historical
    JSON value from a 1.2.1 host cannot decode the answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    The reverse direction is fine. An older client reading from a newer host works, because the
    shape it already knew is still there.
  </p>

  <p>
    So: run the same version on both sides of a broker. This is tracked upstream as strata-core
    issue 3369. A related one is worth pairing with it, strata-core issue 3395: a client that asks
    for
    <code>--durability always</code> against a host running the default gets the host's mode, and the
    flag is accepted silently. <a href="/docs/learn/durability">Durability</a> covers what that costs.
  </p>

  <h2>What a research preview promises</h2>

  <p>
    Plainly: the command surface is still moving. Commands have been added and renamed between minor
    releases, output formats changed shape in 1.2.2, and a capability enum was renamed in the same
    release. None of that is finished.
  </p>

  <p>What is stable enough to build on:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The error codes.</strong> Stable identifiers with a retry policy and a commit outcome per
      code, which is what a program should branch on. See <a href="/docs/reference/errors">
        Error codes
      </a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The <code>--json</code> envelope.</strong> It did not change when the human output was rewritten
      in 1.2.2. It is the format to parse.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Format 1 on disk.</strong> Unchanged across every 1.x release so far.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>What is not:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The human output. It is for reading, and 1.2.2 rewrote it wholesale.</li>
    <li>Command names and flags, which are still being added to.</li>
    <li>Node.js and Rust, which do not ship.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Upgrading</h2>

  <p>
    <code>strata update</code> replaces the binary in place. Because every 1.x release reads format 1,
    there is no migration step and no downgrade hazard within 1.x today. Back up first anyway if the data
    matters: <a href="/docs/learn/databases-and-storage">Databases and storage</a> covers what is safe
    to copy and when.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
